# Scope and identity

Scope: reassess an existing span before refreshing it using scope and identity.

## When to use this type

A name, key or path could discard context needed to distinguish or equate entities. Random uniqueness alone does not establish stable identity, correct binding or reproducible selection.

## Information and anchors

State the key recipe, partition/binding/invocation dimensions, intentional sharing and aliases that denote the same entity.

Candidate anchor roles: context or identity constructor; lookup/deduplication consumer; reset owner; separation/equivalence example. Select existing content carrying the rule or likely to be encountered at the risky decision. These are roles, not a required anchor count.

## Reassess and reconcile

1. Use the current why, anchor bytes and history from stage 3 of [the procedure](../procedure.md). Check whether changed keys, caches, paths or symbol matching lose a dimension or introduce unwanted fragmentation.
2. Confirm authority and meaning before refreshing content. Preserve unresolved disagreements; return behavior findings to the owner.
3. Select evidence appropriate to the semantic change: Use two equal-looking entities that must remain separate and, where relevant, two aliases that must collapse; interleave partitioned state.
4. Refresh unchanged identities or atomically replace moved paths/ranges under the procedure. Revise the why when its rule or scope changes; extend, split or consolidate only when the information requires it.
5. Retain useful explanation even when tested or reduced to one anchor. Retire only when the relationship/reminder is obsolete or adds no useful decision context. Require scoped zero drift; delegated work stays unstaged for the owner.

## Illustrative why

> Invocation output identity includes the parent call path; the same task under different parents does not share an output directory.

This example supplies no authority for another repository. Substitute its actual contract and scope; quote decisive requirement wording where available.

